/* book exam traps: A normal SpO₂ does not prove good breathing. A patient on oxygen can hypoventilate and retain carbon dioxide while the saturation looks fine. | R rises as saturation falls. Do not invert the ratio or read the calibration table backwards. | AC is the pulsatile arterial part; DC is the steady tissue, bone and venous part. Swapping them destroys the measurement. | The figure 1 Ω per litre is a typical adult value, not a fixed constant. Electrode position, chest shape and lung volume change it. | Carboxyhaemoglobin makes SpO₂ falsely high; dark nail polish and methaemoglobin make it unreliable. Motion and low perfusion make the ratio noisy, not just the waveform ugly. */
