/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The box is rigid and sealed, so the gas inside has a fixed mass.
   - With the shutter closed the subject pants gently at about 1 Hz.
   - Apply Boyle's law to the trapped lung gas.
   - Vtg is the thoracic gas volume; ΔV is the lung volume change read from the calibrated box; PB is barometric pressure and PH₂O = 47 mmHg, both converted to cm H₂O (760 mmHg = 1033 cm H₂O, 47 mmHg = 64 cm H₂O, so PB − PH₂O ≈ 970 cm H₂O at sea
   - If the shutter closes at the end of a quiet breath out, Vtg is the functional residual capacity, FRC.
   - The gas methods measure only gas that talks to the airway.
   - With the shutter open the subject keeps panting, now through the pneumotachometer.
   - Raw is airway resistance in cm H₂O per L/s; the first ratio is the slope of the closed-shutter trace; the second is the box swing per unit flow V̇ (in L/s) in the open-shutter trace.
   - Normal adult airway resistance is roughly 0.6 to 2.4 cm H₂O per L/s; higher values suggest obstruction.
*/
